<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0"
         xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 https://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
  <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>
  <parent>
    <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
    <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-parent</artifactId>
    <version>4.1.0</version>
    <relativePath/>
  </parent>
  <groupId>com.ffroliva</groupId>
  <artifactId>tiny-ledger</artifactId>
  <version>0.1.0-SNAPSHOT</version>
  <properties>
    <java.version>25</java.version>
    <spring-modulith.version>2.1.0</spring-modulith.version>
    <testcontainers.version>1.20.5</testcontainers.version>
    <archunit.version>1.5.0</archunit.version>
    <cucumber.version>7.34.6</cucumber.version>
    <openapi-generator.version>7.24.0</openapi-generator.version>
    <!-- AGENTS.md trap 4: surefire's failIfNoSpecifiedTests defaults to FALSE, so `-Dtest=Typo`
         matches nothing and the build exits 0 - a red step can "pass" having executed nothing, which
         this repository has already shipped once. Only applies when `-Dtest` is given, so full runs
         are unaffected. Failsafe's equivalent is configured on the plugin in the `it` profile. -->
    <surefire.failIfNoSpecifiedTests>true</surefire.failIfNoSpecifiedTests>

    <!-- SonarCloud. These live here rather than in the workflow so `sonar:sonar` is reproducible
         locally with a token, which is this pipeline's stated rule for a gate (§12.1's closing note).
         Only the token is a secret.

         The organization is `ffroliva-github`, NOT `ffroliva`. SonarCloud appends `-github` when an
         organization is created from a GitHub account, while the PROJECT key keeps the plain
         `owner_repo` form — so the two do not match, which is exactly the trap. Read from
         `api/organizations/search?member=true` after the first attempt failed with
         "Organization key 'ffroliva' does not exist", rather than guessed a second time. -->
    <!-- OVERRIDES spring-boot-dependencies 4.1.0, which manages postgresql.version at 42.7.11.
         Found by the Trivy gate on its very first honest run (#11, CI 31229592166): CVE-2026-54291,
         HIGH, status `fixed`, installed 42.7.11, fixed 42.7.12 — a pgjdbc man-in-the-middle via
         com.ongres.scram/scram-client. Reported at two locations in the image (runner.jar and the
         workspace lib), which is why the total read 2 for one CVE.

         FIXED RATHER THAN SUPPRESSED, and that distinction is the whole value of the gate: it is
         `fixed` upstream, so `ignore-unfixed: true` did NOT hide it, and an entry in
         .github/owasp-suppressions.xml would have been the wrong answer to an available upgrade.
         42.7.13 is the current release of the same patch line — taking 42.7.12, the literal minimum
         that clears today's scan, would just invite a second bump.

         The OS layer scanned clean in the same run (ubuntu 24.04, 0 findings), so this is the only
         thing standing between the image and a green security job. -->
    <postgresql.version>42.7.13</postgresql.version>

    <!-- OVERRIDES spring-boot-dependencies 4.1.0 (which manages tomcat.version at 11.0.22).
         Surfaced by the first Dependency-Check run (#11, CI 31233407733), which failed with 22
         CVEs at CVSS >= 7 across four libraries. This is the one with a clean, stable patch
         upgrade, so it is taken:  11.0.22 -> 11.0.24.

         THE OTHER THREE ARE DELIBERATELY NOT "FIXED", and the reasons differ:

           netty-transport 4.2.15.Final  — next release is 5.0.0.Alpha2
           kotlin-stdlib   2.3.21        — next release is 2.4.20-Beta2

         Taking an alpha or a beta into a money path to quieten a scanner is a worse decision
         than the finding it silences.

           opentelemetry-semconv 1.41.1  — arrives transitively and is managed by NO Boot
                                           property, so a <opentelemetry-semconv.version>
                                           override would be SILENTLY IGNORED. It was written,
                                           checked against Boot's BOM, found absent, and removed
                                           rather than shipped as a line that does nothing.

         The residue is RECORDED, not suppressed. See §12.1 stage 11b. -->
    <tomcat.version>11.0.24</tomcat.version>

    <sonar.host.url>https://sonarcloud.io</sonar.host.url>
    <sonar.organization>ffroliva-github</sonar.organization>
    <sonar.projectKey>ffroliva_tiny-ledger</sonar.projectKey>
    <sonar-maven-plugin.version>5.1.0.4751</sonar-maven-plugin.version>

    <!-- TWO paths, comma-separated, and that is the whole reason the `it` profile writes its JaCoCo
         data to a separate file. CI runs unit and integration on different runners, so neither ever
         sees the other's coverage; Sonar accepts several reports and unions them. A single path here
         would report the unit suite's coverage as if it were the project's, which is exactly the kind
         of number this repository refuses to publish. Missing files are ignored, so a local
         `verify` (no ITs) still analyses cleanly with unit coverage alone. -->
    <sonar.coverage.jacoco.xmlReportPaths>
      ${project.build.directory}/site/jacoco/jacoco.xml,${project.build.directory}/site/jacoco-it/jacoco.xml
    </sonar.coverage.jacoco.xmlReportPaths>
  </properties>
  <dependencyManagement>
    <dependencies>
      <dependency>
        <groupId>org.springframework.modulith</groupId>
        <artifactId>spring-modulith-bom</artifactId>
        <version>${spring-modulith.version}</version>
        <type>pom</type>
        <scope>import</scope>
      </dependency>
      <dependency>
        <groupId>org.testcontainers</groupId>
        <artifactId>testcontainers-bom</artifactId>
        <version>${testcontainers.version}</version>
        <type>pom</type>
        <scope>import</scope>
      </dependency>
    </dependencies>
  </dependencyManagement>
  <dependencies>
    <dependency><groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId><artifactId>spring-boot-starter-web</artifactId></dependency>
    <dependency><groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId><artifactId>spring-boot-starter-validation</artifactId></dependency>
    <!-- Spec §6.6 / ADR 0004: liveness and readiness probes, and the Micrometer MeterRegistry the
         ledger.outbox.pending.age.seconds gauge registers on. Exposure is locked to `health` in
         application.properties and denied a second time in SecurityConfig — see the endpoint
         assessment in docs/superpowers/plans/2026-08-07-step-9-probes-and-lag-gauge.md. -->
    <dependency><groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId><artifactId>spring-boot-starter-actuator</artifactId></dependency>
    <!-- Spec §6.6 / §14 step 9 part 2. ONE starter, not four: read out of the published POM,
         spring-boot-starter-opentelemetry (4.1.0) brings micrometer-registry-otlp,
         micrometer-tracing-bridge-otel and opentelemetry-exporter-otlp, plus
         spring-boot-micrometer-tracing-opentelemetry and spring-boot-opentelemetry. Every version
         comes from the Boot BOM (micrometer 1.17.0, micrometer-tracing 1.7.0, opentelemetry 1.62.0),
         so nothing is pinned here.

         Micrometer Tracing over the OTel BRIDGE, never the OTel Java agent (ADR 0005): domain spans
         are written explicitly and stay reviewable in the source. Export is off by default in
         application.properties — the classpath decides what CAN be exported, the properties decide
         whether anything IS. -->
    <dependency><groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId><artifactId>spring-boot-starter-opentelemetry</artifactId></dependency>
    <!-- Spec §1: security is one mechanism with two configurations, not a dependency excluded in
         standalone. SecurityConfig declares a profile-scoped chain for each mode. -->
    <dependency><groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId><artifactId>spring-boot-starter-security</artifactId></dependency>
    <dependency><groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId><artifactId>spring-boot-starter-oauth2-resource-server</artifactId></dependency>
    <dependency><groupId>org.springframework.security</groupId><artifactId>spring-security-test</artifactId><scope>test</scope></dependency>
    <!-- §9.4's InMemorySpanExporter. Deliberately NOT spring-boot-starter-opentelemetry-test: that
         module carries @AutoConfigureTracing and @AutoConfigureMetrics and no in-memory exporter at
         all, and both annotations change a test's context cache key (AGENTS.md trap 5). The same
         effect is reached by a property — see AbstractIntegrationTest. -->
    <dependency><groupId>io.opentelemetry</groupId><artifactId>opentelemetry-sdk-testing</artifactId><scope>test</scope></dependency>
    <!-- SimpleTracer/SimpleSpan: lets TracedUseCasesTest assert span names and tags with no Spring
         context and no OTel SDK. Version from the Boot BOM's micrometer-tracing-bom import. -->
    <dependency><groupId>io.micrometer</groupId><artifactId>micrometer-tracing-test</artifactId><scope>test</scope></dependency>
    <!-- JdbcTemplate only — no JPA anywhere in the adapters, so the JPA starter would just drag
         Hibernate and an eager EntityManagerFactory into the standalone profile. -->
    <dependency><groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId><artifactId>spring-boot-starter-jdbc</artifactId></dependency>
    <dependency><groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId><artifactId>spring-boot-starter-data-redis</artifactId></dependency>
    <!-- Spec §6.1: token buckets. Storage is the part that swaps by profile (RateLimitConfig) —
         bucket4j-core alone runs the local, in-memory `standalone` bucket; the two redis artifacts
         add Bucket4jLettuce's distributed proxy for `full`, reusing the lettuce-core already on the
         classpath via spring-boot-starter-data-redis rather than adding a second Redis client. -->
    <dependency><groupId>com.bucket4j</groupId><artifactId>bucket4j_jdk17-core</artifactId><version>8.19.0</version></dependency>
    <dependency><groupId>com.bucket4j</groupId><artifactId>bucket4j_jdk17-redis-common</artifactId><version>8.19.0</version></dependency>
    <dependency><groupId>com.bucket4j</groupId><artifactId>bucket4j_jdk17-lettuce</artifactId><version>8.19.0</version></dependency>
    <!-- Spec §6.1: the bounded, expiring store backing standalone's per-key buckets — version
         supplied by spring-boot-starter-parent's dependency management, same as every other
         Boot-covered dependency in this file. -->
    <dependency><groupId>com.github.ben-manes.caffeine</groupId><artifactId>caffeine</artifactId></dependency>
    <dependency><groupId>org.springframework.kafka</groupId><artifactId>spring-kafka</artifactId></dependency>
    <!-- Boot 4 ships Liquibase auto-config in its own module; bare liquibase-core means the
         changelog never runs and the full profile boots against an empty schema. -->
    <dependency><groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId><artifactId>spring-boot-starter-liquibase</artifactId></dependency>
    <dependency><groupId>org.postgresql</groupId><artifactId>postgresql</artifactId><scope>runtime</scope></dependency>
    <dependency><groupId>org.springframework.modulith</groupId><artifactId>spring-modulith-starter-core</artifactId></dependency>
    <!-- ADR 0001: Modulith owns the event-publication registry (JDBC) and the Kafka relay;
         there is no hand-rolled outbox. -->
    <dependency><groupId>org.springframework.modulith</groupId><artifactId>spring-modulith-starter-jdbc</artifactId></dependency>
    <dependency><groupId>org.springframework.modulith</groupId><artifactId>spring-modulith-events-kafka</artifactId></dependency>
    <dependency><groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId><artifactId>spring-boot-starter-test</artifactId><scope>test</scope></dependency>
    <!-- Boot 4 moved the @WebMvcTest slice out of spring-boot-test-autoconfigure into its own module;
         spring-boot-starter-test no longer carries it (spec §9.5). -->
    <dependency><groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId><artifactId>spring-boot-webmvc-test</artifactId><scope>test</scope></dependency>
    <dependency><groupId>org.springframework.modulith</groupId><artifactId>spring-modulith-starter-test</artifactId><scope>test</scope></dependency>
    <dependency><groupId>org.testcontainers</groupId><artifactId>junit-jupiter</artifactId><scope>test</scope></dependency>
    <dependency><groupId>org.testcontainers</groupId><artifactId>postgresql</artifactId><scope>test</scope></dependency>
    <dependency><groupId>org.testcontainers</groupId><artifactId>kafka</artifactId><scope>test</scope></dependency>
    <dependency><groupId>com.redis</groupId><artifactId>testcontainers-redis</artifactId><version>2.2.4</version><scope>test</scope></dependency>
    <dependency><groupId>com.tngtech.archunit</groupId><artifactId>archunit-junit5</artifactId><version>${archunit.version}</version><scope>test</scope></dependency>
    <dependency><groupId>io.cucumber</groupId><artifactId>cucumber-java</artifactId><version>${cucumber.version}</version><scope>test</scope></dependency>
    <dependency><groupId>io.cucumber</groupId><artifactId>cucumber-junit-platform-engine</artifactId><version>${cucumber.version}</version><scope>test</scope></dependency>
    <dependency><groupId>io.cucumber</groupId><artifactId>cucumber-spring</artifactId><version>${cucumber.version}</version><scope>test</scope></dependency>
    <dependency><groupId>org.junit.platform</groupId><artifactId>junit-platform-suite</artifactId><scope>test</scope></dependency>
    <dependency><groupId>org.awaitility</groupId><artifactId>awaitility</artifactId><scope>test</scope></dependency>
  </dependencies>
  <build>
    <plugins>
      <!--
        The `exec` classifier makes the REPACKAGED jar tiny-ledger-<version>-exec.jar and leaves the
        plain, ordinary jar as the main artifact. Without it, `repackage` replaces the main artifact
        with a Boot jar whose classes live under BOOT-INF/classes/ — a layout javac cannot read — so
        anything depending on this project compiles against nothing:

            cannot find symbol: class Money

        That is exactly what benchmarks/ hit, and it is the documented reason Boot ships the
        classifier option. Consumers now get real classes; the runnable jar is still produced, just
        named -exec (see scripts/e2e/run-e2e.sh).
      -->
      <plugin><groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId><artifactId>spring-boot-maven-plugin</artifactId>
        <configuration>
          <classifier>exec</classifier>
          <!--
            Issue #11 / spec §12. Buildpacks, not a Dockerfile: the image is layered by construction
            and there is no base image for this repository to patch and forget.

            `image.name` is pinned to a local, unqualified name ON PURPOSE. Nothing publishes it —
            stage 12 is a separate, unbuilt decision (§12.1) — so a registry-qualified name here would
            imply a push that does not happen.

            The classifier above is why this needs saying: build-image consumes the REPACKAGED jar,
            and this project deliberately gives that jar an `exec` classifier so the plain jar stays
            usable as a dependency by benchmarks/. Getting this wrong produces an image that builds
            cleanly and fails at startup with a missing main class.

            NO <executions>: nothing binds build-image to a lifecycle phase, for the same reason
            sonar-maven-plugin and dependency-check-maven below carry none. `verify` must start ZERO
            containers (ADR 0003) and build-image drives a Docker daemon build.
          -->
          <image>
            <name>tiny-ledger:${project.version}</name>
            <env>
              <!--
                Spec v3.36 / #11. The CURRENT names — BP_JVM_CDS_ENABLED and
                CDS_TRAINING_JAVA_TOOL_OPTIONS are both DEPRECATED aliases (Paketo spring-boot
                buildpack README, read 2026-08-08). Same trap as step 9's
                management.otlp.tracing.*: the spelling in common circulation is the dead one, and a
                deprecated or misspelled name is SILENTLY IGNORED — indistinguishable from not
                setting it at all.
              -->
              <BP_JVM_AOTCACHE_ENABLED>true</BP_JVM_AOTCACHE_ENABLED>
              <!--
                THE TRAINING RUN STARTS THE APPLICATION. Under `full` it would block on Postgres,
                Redis, Kafka and Liquibase and hang the build — predicted in spec v3.36 before this
                was ever run, and the buildpack's own documentation for this variable says it exists
                "to configure your app not to reach external services during" the run.

                `standalone` is the whole answer, and it is available precisely because §1 keeps two
                run modes in one codebase. The duality earns its keep here in a way nobody planned.
              -->
              <TRAINING_RUN_JAVA_TOOL_OPTIONS>-Dspring.profiles.active=standalone</TRAINING_RUN_JAVA_TOOL_OPTIONS>
            </env>
          </image>
        </configuration></plugin>
      <plugin>
        <groupId>org.openapitools</groupId><artifactId>openapi-generator-maven-plugin</artifactId><version>${openapi-generator.version}</version>
        <executions><execution><goals><goal>generate</goal></goals>
          <configuration>
            <inputSpec>docs/api/openapi.yaml</inputSpec>
            <generatorName>spring</generatorName>
            <configOptions>
              <interfaceOnly>true</interfaceOnly>
              <useSpringBoot3>true</useSpringBoot3>
              <apiPackage>com.ffroliva.tinyledger.api.generated.api</apiPackage>
              <modelPackage>com.ffroliva.tinyledger.api.generated.model</modelPackage>
              <useTags>true</useTags>
              <!-- No swagger-annotations and no jackson-databind-nullable on the classpath; keeping
                   the generated sources dependency-free is cheaper than adding either (spec §5). -->
              <documentationProvider>none</documentationProvider>
              <annotationLibrary>none</annotationLibrary>
              <openApiNullable>false</openApiNullable>
            </configOptions>
          </configuration>
        </execution></executions>
      </plugin>
      <plugin>
        <groupId>com.diffplug.spotless</groupId><artifactId>spotless-maven-plugin</artifactId><version>3.9.0</version>
        <configuration><java><palantirJavaFormat/></java></configuration>
        <executions><execution><goals><goal>check</goal></goals></execution></executions>
      </plugin>
      <!-- Version pinned here and nowhere else, so `./mvnw sonar:sonar` resolves the same scanner in
           CI and on a laptop. No <executions>: nothing binds it to a phase, because analysis is a
           deliberate invocation and must never ride along with `verify`. -->
      <plugin>
        <groupId>org.sonarsource.scanner.maven</groupId>
        <artifactId>sonar-maven-plugin</artifactId>
        <version>${sonar-maven-plugin.version}</version>
      </plugin>
      <!--
        OWASP Dependency-Check 13.0.0 (issue #11, stage 11). NO <executions>, exactly like
        sonar-maven-plugin above and for the same stated reason: this is a deliberate invocation and
        must never ride along with `verify`. It downloads the NVD feed; bound to a phase it would
        make every local build slow and network-dependent, and `verify` starting to need the internet
        is a change nobody asked for.

        WHY IT EARNS ITS PLACE ALONGSIDE TRIVY, stated honestly rather than as "Trivy was not
        enough": Trivy scans the IMAGE, so it sees runtime jars and OS packages. Dependency-Check
        scans the BUILD, so it also sees TEST-scope dependencies. That is the only real gap between
        them and it is a small one — belt and braces, chosen deliberately.

        THE NVD KEY IS A COMMAND-LINE PROPERTY AND ITS NAME IS `nvdApiKey`. Read out of this
        plugin's own META-INF/maven/plugin.xml at 13.0.0, which declares
        `<nvdApiKey implementation="java.lang.String">${nvdApiKey}</nvdApiKey>` — so the invocation
        is `-DnvdApiKey=...`. The #11 plan said `-Dnvd.api.key` and that is WRONG: an unrecognised
        -D is silently ignored, so the build would simply rate-limit against an anonymous NVD and
        look like a slow network rather than a misconfiguration. Same shape as the deprecated
        buildpack variables above and step 9's management.otlp.tracing.*.
      -->
      <plugin>
        <groupId>org.owasp</groupId>
        <artifactId>dependency-check-maven</artifactId>
        <version>13.0.0</version>
        <configuration>
          <!-- THE GATE. The plugin's default is 11, which no CVSS score can reach — that is a
               reporter, not a gate, and shipping it would be the THIRD check here that cannot fail
               (the deleted stage 6, then the Sonar job before -Dsonar.qualitygate.wait=true).
               7.0 is the bottom of CVSS v3 HIGH. -->
          <failBuildOnCVSS>7</failBuildOnCVSS>
          <suppressionFiles>
            <suppressionFile>.github/owasp-suppressions.xml</suppressionFile>
          </suppressionFiles>
          <!-- The retired/experimental analysers add runtime and noise for a Java-only project. -->
          <nodeAnalyzerEnabled>false</nodeAnalyzerEnabled>
          <assemblyAnalyzerEnabled>false</assemblyAnalyzerEnabled>
        </configuration>
      </plugin>
      <plugin>
        <groupId>org.jacoco</groupId><artifactId>jacoco-maven-plugin</artifactId><version>0.8.15</version>
        <executions>
          <execution><goals><goal>prepare-agent</goal></goals></execution>
          <!-- Declared BEFORE `check` on purpose: both default to the `verify` phase, and within one
               phase Maven runs executions in declaration order. Sonar consumes this XML; nothing did
               before, so no report goal was bound at all. -->
          <execution><id>report</id><goals><goal>report</goal></goals></execution>
          <execution><id>check</id><goals><goal>check</goal></goals>
            <configuration><rules><rule>
              <element>PACKAGE</element>
              <includes><include>com.ffroliva.tinyledger.*.domain*</include></includes>
              <limits>
                <limit><counter>LINE</counter><value>COVEREDRATIO</value><minimum>0.90</minimum></limit>
                <limit><counter>BRANCH</counter><value>COVEREDRATIO</value><minimum>0.85</minimum></limit>
              </limits>
            </rule></rules></configuration>
          </execution>
        </executions>
      </plugin>
    </plugins>
  </build>
  <profiles>
    <profile>
      <!-- *IT.java suites need Docker (Testcontainers: Postgres, Redis, Kafka, Keycloak). Surefire's
           default includes already skip them, so plain `./mvnw verify` stays the fast unit pipeline;
           `./mvnw verify -Pit` adds failsafe to run them. -->
      <id>it</id>
      <properties>
        <!-- JaCoCo's COVERAGE GATE measures the UNIT suite, which this profile skips - so with no
             surefire coverage data the check fails on ~0% and the integration job dies before
             failsafe reports anything. Measured: CI run 31078... failed exactly there.
             The `unit` job still enforces the gates on every push; running them a second time
             against the same code was the duplication this profile change exists to remove.
             That reasoning is about the `check` GOAL, and `jacoco.skip=true` used to switch off the
             whole plugin - including the agent, so `-Pit` produced no coverage data at all and the
             integration suite was invisible to any coverage report. The `check` execution is now
             disabled on its own (phase `none`, below) and collection is left on, which keeps the
             original decision exactly and stops it costing more than it was meant to. -->
        <jacoco.destFile>${project.build.directory}/jacoco-it.exec</jacoco.destFile>
      </properties>
      <build>
        <plugins>
          <plugin>
            <groupId>org.jacoco</groupId>
            <artifactId>jacoco-maven-plugin</artifactId>
            <configuration>
              <!-- The agent writes here (jacoco.destFile above) and the report reads the same file
                   into its own directory, so the unit run's target/jacoco.exec and
                   target/site/jacoco are never overwritten. That matters beyond CI, where the two
                   run on separate runners: locally, `verify` then `verify -Pit` in the same tree
                   would otherwise clobber the first result with the second - the collision AGENTS.md
                   already warns about for two concurrent Maven builds. -->
              <dataFile>${project.build.directory}/jacoco-it.exec</dataFile>
              <outputDirectory>${project.build.directory}/site/jacoco-it</outputDirectory>
            </configuration>
            <executions>
              <!-- The gate, and only the gate. See the property comment above for why it cannot run
                   here: it would measure a suite this profile deliberately skipped. -->
              <execution><id>check</id><phase>none</phase></execution>
            </executions>
          </plugin>
          <plugin>
            <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
            <artifactId>maven-surefire-plugin</artifactId>
            <configuration>
              <!-- Skip the unit suite under -Pit. `verify` runs surefire then failsafe, so without
                   this the integration job re-ran every unit test the unit job had just run - about
                   90s of the integration job's 200s, on every push, for a result already known.
                   `skipTests` skips execution but still COMPILES the tests (maven.test.skip would
                   skip compilation and break the failsafe classpath). The unit job counts
                   surefire-reports and the integration job counts failsafe-reports, so neither
                   count gate is affected. -->
              <skipTests>true</skipTests>
            </configuration>
          </plugin>
          <plugin>
            <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
            <artifactId>maven-failsafe-plugin</artifactId>
            <configuration>
              <includes>
                <include>**/*IT.java</include>
              </includes>
              <!-- AGENTS.md trap 4: this defaults to FALSE, so `-Dit.test=Typo` matches nothing and
                   exits 0 - a targeted run that proves nothing while looking green. Only applies
                   when a filter is given, so full runs are unaffected. -->
              <failIfNoSpecifiedTests>true</failIfNoSpecifiedTests>
            </configuration>
            <executions>
              <execution>
                <goals>
                  <goal>integration-test</goal>
                  <goal>verify</goal>
                </goals>
              </execution>
            </executions>
          </plugin>
        </plugins>
      </build>
    </profile>
    <profile>
      <!-- Report-only mutation coverage on the domain and use cases. Not wired into `verify` and
           carries no threshold on purpose - see docs/performance-findings.md §4. Run explicitly:
           `./mvnw -Pmutation org.pitest:pitest-maven:mutationCoverage`. -->
      <id>mutation</id>
      <build>
        <plugins>
          <plugin>
            <groupId>org.pitest</groupId>
            <artifactId>pitest-maven</artifactId>
            <!-- Newest available on Maven Central as of this task (checked via maven-metadata.xml;
                 the plan's suggested 1.20.4 is several releases behind). Confirmed it loads on
                 JDK 25: `./mvnw org.pitest:pitest-maven:help` succeeds. -->
            <version>1.25.9</version>
            <dependencies>
              <dependency>
                <groupId>org.pitest</groupId>
                <artifactId>pitest-junit5-plugin</artifactId>
                <version>1.2.3</version>
              </dependency>
            </dependencies>
            <configuration>
              <!-- The domain and the use cases only. A surviving mutant in Account or Money is a
                   missing test; a surviving mutant in a Spring adapter is usually just wiring. -->
              <targetClasses>
                <param>com.ffroliva.tinyledger.ledger.domain.*</param>
                <param>com.ffroliva.tinyledger.ledger.application.*</param>
                <param>com.ffroliva.tinyledger.shared.*</param>
              </targetClasses>
              <targetTests>
                <param>com.ffroliva.tinyledger.*Test</param>
              </targetTests>
              <outputFormats>
                <param>HTML</param>
                <param>XML</param>
              </outputFormats>
              <!-- No threshold yet, on purpose: measure before gating. -->
              <timestampedReports>false</timestampedReports>
            </configuration>
          </plugin>
        </plugins>
      </build>
    </profile>
  </profiles>
</project>
